# Navigate the robot to a pose, and say WHY when it cannot.
#
# Served by task_runner (syncai_task_runner, SyncAI-Robot-Workspace) on the
# relative name `navigate_to_goal` -> <robot_id>/navigate_to_goal, next to the
# UNCHANGED nav2_msgs/NavigateToPose on <robot_id>/navigate_to_pose: same
# navigator code, same behavior tree (move.xml), same preemption rules; only
# the result and one feedback field differ. Added 2026-10 because the
# backend's MOVE step got ABORTED with an empty std_msgs/Empty result and
# could not tell the operator whether the robot failed to plan or failed to
# drive. The backend migrates from NavigateToPose at its own pace; the old
# action stays until it has.
#
# Field NAMES mirror nav2's NavigateToPose on purpose (goal.pose,
# goal.behavior_tree, the five feedback fields): one navigator template in
# task_runner serves both actions, and it compiles only because the member
# names agree. Rename a field here and that template needs a trait.

# Target pose, in the navigator's global_frame (map).
geometry_msgs/PoseStamped pose
# Absolute path of a BT XML to tick instead of the default move.xml; empty =
# the default. A new goal sent while one runs is accepted in place (no
# restart, no replan cycle) only if it names the same tree; otherwise it is
# terminated and the running goal continues.
string behavior_tree
---
# Which stage failed, coarsely. Meaningful ONLY with status ABORTED;
# SUCCEEDED and CANCELED carry NONE.
#
# ABORTED + NONE is not a navigation failure. It means the goal was
# superseded by a newer goal on this action (preempted in place: the old goal
# is aborted with a default result), or was rejected before the tree ran --
# the other navigator in task_runner was busy (navigate_to_pose and
# navigate_to_goal share one robot, so one navigation at a time), or the
# requested behavior_tree file could not be loaded. The task_runner log has
# which.
#
# Layout, nav2 Iron's convention, so finer codes slot in without renumbering:
#   0        none
#   1..99    navigator / tree level
#   100..199 the path-following branch (FollowPath -> syncai_controller)
#   200..299 the planning branch (ComputePathToPose -> syncai_planner)
# The two branch constants are their range's base. Humble's nav2_msgs
# results carry no reason, so today the branch is all the stack knows; when
# a finer reason exists it becomes base + n (Iron: 104 patience exceeded,
# 207 no valid path, ...) and a client that switches on the hundreds keeps
# working. The bases are a contract between this file and the backend:
# never renumber them.
uint16 NONE=0
# The tree failed without a node reporting: an exception inside the tree, or
# a branch whose server never acknowledged the goal (error_msg names it).
uint16 UNKNOWN=1
# The path-following branch failed the tree, after move.xml's own retries:
# blocked with no detour, no progress for movement_time_allowance, or the
# robot pose lost for longer than failure_tolerance.
uint16 FOLLOW_PATH_FAILED=100
# The planning branch failed the tree: no route, start or goal inside an
# obstacle / keepout zone, or a TF failure -- twice, since the tree clears
# the global costmap and retries once before giving up.
uint16 PLAN_FAILED=200

uint16 error_code
# Human-readable, written for the operator console: a stage sentence with
# the BT node's detail in parentheses. Empty when error_code is NONE.
string error_msg
---
# nav2's five fields, same names, plus one.
geometry_msgs/PoseStamped current_pose
builtin_interfaces/Duration navigation_time
builtin_interfaces/Duration estimated_time_remaining
int16 number_of_recoveries
float32 distance_remaining
# Plans made for this goal beyond the first: 0 while the first path is still
# good. Since 2026-10 move.xml plans only when the path is blocked or the
# goal changed, so this counts detours, which is what an operator watching a
# stalled robot wants to know. Resets with every (pre-empted) goal.
uint16 number_of_replans
